`,(0,n.jsx)(i,{question:"You plan a feature that drafts release notes from merged pull requests. You haven't built anything yet. What do you write down before you choose how much to build?",options:[{label:"The steps a workflow would run, in their order",feedback:"Listing steps comes later, once a test shows a single request falls short."},{label:"The tools an agent would need for the job",feedback:"Tools matter only if you reach an agent, and nothing shows you need one yet."},{label:"What a good release note looks like",correct:!0,feedback:"With a good result written down, you can test the simplest option and see whether it falls short."}]}),`
`,(0,n.jsx)(r.h2,{id:"start-with-a-single-request",children:"Start with a single request"}),`
`,(0,n.jsxs)(r.p,{children:["A single request is one Messages API request, with one answer back. Along with the ticket, it can carry the help articles your code looks up for that ticket and a few past replies you liked, as examples to follow. ",(0,n.jsx)(r.strong,{children:"For many features, a single request built this way is usually enough."})]}),`
`,(0,n.jsx)(r.p,{children:"To see whether a single request is enough for your tickets, run it on real tickets from last month. Grade each reply against what you wrote down for a good one. Then read the replies that missed, and look at which kinds of tickets they answered. A test like this, with real inputs and a way to grade what comes back, is called an eval."}),`
`,(0,n.jsx)(r.p,{children:"If the replies match what you wrote down, ship the single request and stop there. Where they miss, improve the request's instructions, articles, and examples before you move up."}),`
`,(0,n.jsx)(r.p,{children:"One sign that a single request isn't enough is that a fix for one kind of ticket makes another kind worse. For example, you add your refund rules to the prompt, and the replies to technical questions get worse. When you keep trading one fix for another like this, try a workflow."}),`
`,(0,n.jsx)(i,{question:"A single request drafts release notes from merged pull requests. You run it on last month's releases and grade each note. What result tells you to try a workflow?",options:[{label:"Each fix for one kind of release makes another worse",correct:!0,feedback:"When you keep trading one fix for another, a single request isn't enough, so try a workflow."},{label:"A few notes miss the first time you run the test",feedback:"Where notes miss, improve the request's instructions and examples before you move up."},{label:"Every note passes, but more steps might score higher",feedback:"If the notes match what you wrote down, ship the single request and stop there."}]}),`
`,(0,n.jsx)(r.h2,{id:"move-to-a-workflow-when-you-can-list-the-steps",children:"Move to a workflow when you can list the steps"}),`
