`,(0,n.jsx)(r.p,{children:"A workflow for the support tickets could run in two steps:"}),`
`,(0,n.jsxs)(r.ol,{children:[`
`,(0,n.jsx)(r.li,{children:"A first request sorts the ticket into one of three kinds: refund, technical question, or general question."}),`
`,(0,n.jsx)(r.li,{children:"Your code sends the ticket in a second request, with the prompt and help articles for that kind of ticket."}),`
`]}),`
`,(0,n.jsx)(r.p,{children:"Each kind of ticket now has its own prompt, so a change to the refund prompt doesn't touch the technical replies."}),`
`,(0,n.jsx)(r.p,{children:"Splitting a task into requests also lets you see what comes out between steps. Each step is its own request, so you can log it, grade it, and check it before the next one runs. For example, your code can check that the first request returned one of the three kinds, and send any other answer to a person."}),`
`,(0,n.jsx)(r.p,{children:"This workflow makes your customers wait longer, because the second request can't start until the first one finishes. You accept that wait to get more accurate replies, and your test has to show that you get them."}),`
`,(0,n.jsx)(r.p,{children:"A workflow no longer fits when you can't write the steps down ahead of time. For example, a reply needs Claude to look up the customer's order, and what it checks next depends on what it finds there."}),`
`,(0,n.jsx)(r.h2,{id:"move-to-an-agent-only-when-you-cant",children:"Move to an agent only when you can't"}),`
`,(0,n.jsx)(r.p,{children:"Choose an agent only for an open-ended task, where Claude has to pick each next step with the tools you give it. Open-ended means it's hard or impossible to predict how many steps the task needs, and you can't write the steps down ahead of time."}),`
`,(0,n.jsx)(r.p,{children:"For example, a customer writes that their parcel never arrived. Claude looks up their order history first and finds that the parcel went to an old address. So Claude reads your policy articles on wrong addresses next. Then it issues a refund or updates the ticket."}),`
`,(0,n.jsxs)(r.p,{children:[(0,n.jsx)(r.strong,{children:"An agent costs more, and its mistakes can build on each other."})," The freedom to choose that makes an agent useful also makes it harder to test. It may run for many steps, so you need some trust in its decisions."]}),`
`,(0,n.jsx)(r.p,{children:"Before you choose an agent, answer these questions about the task:"}),`
`,(0,n.jsxs)(r.ul,{children:[`
`,(0,n.jsx)(r.li,{children:"Is a better result worth the higher cost and the longer wait?"}),`
`,(0,n.jsx)(r.li,{children:"Does Claude do this kind of task well?"}),`
`,(0,n.jsx)(r.li,{children:"Can a wrong step be caught and recovered from, with tests, a review, or a rollback?"}),`
`]}),`
`,(0,n.jsx)(r.p,{children:"If the answer to any of them is no, stay with a workflow or a single request. For the support tickets, that means you keep the workflow for the kinds of tickets whose steps you can list, and send the rest to a person."}),`
